<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Cambay State</h1><p class="st-meta">1730 CE – 1948 CE (approx.) · capital Khambhat</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Muslim-ruled port state on the Gulf of Khambhat, founded by the last Mughal governors of Gujarat as the old harbour silted up.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/momin-khan-i">Mirza Ja'far Najm-ud-daula</a>, titled Momin Khan I, was a Mughal official of Persian descent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>. As imperial authority collapsed he made himself master of the ancient port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khambhat">Cambay</a> (Khambhat) around 1730, founding a line of Nawabs who survived <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> pressure by paying them a share of the revenue.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khambhat">Cambay</a> had been one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India's</a> great ports, but silting of the gulf reduced it to a modest town known for agate and cornelian working, textiles and salt. The Nawabs, who were Shia, ruled a mostly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> population and accepted British protection in the late 18th century, later belonging to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat-states-agency">Gujarat States Agency</a> with an 11-gun salute.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The last Nawab acceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and in 1948 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khambhat">Cambay</a> was merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bombay-state">Bombay State</a>. Since 1960 Khambhat has been part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anand-district">Anand district</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/cambay-state.jpg" alt="Cambay State" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Dr. Andreas Birken · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cambayflag.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1730 CE – 1948 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Khambhat</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Momin Khan I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism; Shia Islam (ruling house)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gujarati</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Khambhat</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1839&amp;focus=cambay-state" aria-label="Cambay State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6625 1354 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Cambay State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6945 1610L7364 1601M6945 1610L7364 1602M6945 1610L7018 1386"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7364 1601h0M7364 1602h0M7018 1386h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6945 1610h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6945 1610h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1839&amp;focus=cambay-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
